Traffic Priority marks chosen connections with a DSCP: classes by
interface, address, port and the DSCP a connection arrives with, and by
the groups and users of os-useridentity and the applications and
categories of os-appcontrol, through those plugins' own pf tables. Each
class is written as pf match rules, registered by the plugin at every
filter reload and never added to the owner's own rules. pf writes the
class's DSCP into every packet of its connections, both ways, the ECN
bits kept, fixed as each connection opens. On a Sophos XGS, os-xgs-npu
gives an IPv4 connection in hardware the DSCP pf marked it with. On an
XGS 3300, os-xgs-npu's switch tool gives the first two DSCPs a queue of
their own in the switch, strict or weighted with a minimum, the DSCP of
every other IPv4 connection set to 0 meanwhile, and Firewall: Shaper is
followed into the switch where the switch can do what a rule does; it is
read, never written. Off by default.

